# Leaf `.cargo/config.toml` (RFC-0048 W9). `nros sync` writes these: one
# `include = ["…/nros-patch.toml"]` pointing at a gitignored, host-specific
# central file, plus a `[patch.crates-io]` block of `# nros-managed` path
# entries. A leaf whose config holds nothing else is pure sync output —
# `nros sync` recreates it from scratch — and 54 of them were tracked, churning
# on every sync as the patch set moved.
#
# It cannot be scoped by path: leaves with hand-authored content (`[build]
# target`, a QEMU `runner`, link args) sit in the same directories, and sync
# only refreshes the patch block INSIDE them. Those 90 stay tracked — this rule
# does not touch them, since gitignore has no effect on tracked files — and
# `check-cargo-config-tracked` fails if a config with hand-authored content is
# ever left untracked, so the blanket rule cannot silently swallow a new one.
**/.cargo/config.toml

# Issues 0457/0463 — the HOST-SPECIFIC half of sync's managed
# `[patch.crates-io]`, split out of the leaf `config.toml` above into a file the
# config `include`s.
#
# Only `generated/` rows live here. Those name message crates produced from the
# USER's ament install, so a committed row would assert which ROS install built
# the tree — the rule that already keeps `generated/` itself and the leaf
# `Cargo.lock` out of git.
#
# The IN-REPO rows (`nros-log`, board crates, `mps2-an385-pac`) stay INLINE in
# the tracked config: they are relative paths identical in every checkout, and a
# clone needs them to resolve at all. 0457 moved the whole set here and stranded
# every leaf on `no matching package named 'mps2-an385-pac'`.
#
# Ignoring `config.toml` itself is not an option either — `[build] target`, a
# QEMU `runner` and link args are authored content no generator can reproduce.
# Hence the split is by ORIGIN of the row, not by who wrote it.
#
# A leaf with no message dependency has no sidecar and no `include`, so it
# resolves in a fresh clone with no sync. Where a sidecar IS needed, cargo
# hard-errors on the missing include until `nros sync` runs in that leaf;
# `_require-leaf-includes` is the preflight that says so.
**/.cargo/nros-managed-patch.toml
# Issue 0827 — sync's derived pool budgets, the `[env]` sibling of the file
# above. Derived from the metadata probe's output, which is itself per-host and
# ignored, so committing this would pin one host's answer for everyone.
**/.cargo/nros-managed-env.toml

# Issue 0559 / phase-341 W2 — the per-leaf board `cargo_config` projection
# `nros sync` writes next to the config that `include`s it.
#
# Same blanket shape as `config.toml` above, and safe for the same reason:
# gitignore has no effect on TRACKED files, so the ~41 leaves that commit their
# projection keep it, diffs and all. What this silences is the ~39 leaves whose
# `.cargo/config.toml` is ITSELF generated (and ignored above) — nothing tracked
# references those, a clone never sees them, and sync recreates them on demand.
# They were the entire untracked-file noise floor of `git status` after any
# fixture build.
#
# The rule this cannot express — "…unless a TRACKED config includes you" — is
# enforced instead by `check-cargo-config-tracked`, which fails when a tracked
# config `include`s `nros-board.toml` that is not committed. Without that arm
# this ignore would hide issue 0559 rather than tidy around it: a clone would
# get the include and not the file, and every sync would dirty a tracked config.
**/.cargo/nros-board.toml

# cmake build dirs in the standalone examples. `examples/**/target-*/` above
# covered the cargo side and many leaves carry their own `/build/` line, but
# the west/cmake entry leaves (qemu-arm-freertos, qemu-arm-nuttx,
# threadx-linux) had neither, so 18 build trees showed up untracked after a
# fixture sweep. No `examples/**/build/` path is tracked, here or anywhere.
examples/**/build/

# Phase 277 W7 — standalone example/workspace Cargo.locks are never committed.
# They are not reproducibility-critical (scripts/build/cargo.sh prefetch
# refreshes them without --locked) and committed copies go stale (known-issue
# pattern: locks pinning nros-core 0.1.0 after the 218.J bump).
examples/**/Cargo.lock
/nros-patch.toml
.aider*
examples/workspaces/**/config/*.record.json

# git-stash conflict artifacts (editor/stash-pop debris — a parallel-session
# stash once committed 36 of these; never track them)
*~Stashed changes*
/slides/

# phase-307 W1 — `nros metadata --build` source-metadata sidecars. Generated
# build artifacts (one per Node pkg, `<pkg>/metadata/<component>.json`), derived
# from the pkg's own `Component::register` — never hand-edited, never committed.
examples/**/metadata/*.json
# Same artifact, same writer, other tree: the test bins and benches under
# packages/testing/ are node packages too, so `nros metadata --build` drops
# sidecars beside them. Nothing there is tracked, and a phase-337 wave hit one
# as a stray untracked file. (NOT a blanket `**/metadata/*.json`: the CLI's
# testing workspaces carry a hand-written `talker.json` as a test INPUT —
# see the note on the `.unprobeable` rule below.)
packages/testing/**/metadata/*.json

# Issue #0288's NEGATIVE cache, sibling to those sidecars: when a package's
# probe fails, sync records the source digest as `<sidecar>.unprobeable` so the
# next sync skips a known-failing cargo/cmake build until the sources change,
# and DELETES the marker the moment a probe succeeds. It is a machine-local
# record of a failure observed here — never a fixture, and never the same
# across hosts (the digest mixes in the CLI version, so it moves on every CLI
# rebuild). One had been committed, escaping the `*.json` rule above by ending
# in `.json.unprobeable`, and it churned on every build.
**/metadata/*.unprobeable

# Orphaned generated-header temps. The atomic writers in nros-build-helpers
# stage `.<header>.tmp.<pid>` NEXT TO the destination and then rename it into
# place. A build killed between the write and that rename leaves the temp
# behind — and some destinations are SOURCE dirs (`nros_cpp_ffi.h` is a
# committed header regenerated in place), where a `git add -A` will happily
# commit it. That is exactly how `.nros_cpp_ffi.h.tmp.229021` reached main.
packages/core/*/include/nros/.*.tmp.*
# Catch-all for the same pattern anywhere else a generated header is staged.
.*.tmp.[0-9]*

# Same class, different writer: `nros sync` stages each leaf `.cargo/config.toml`
# as `.config.toml.nros-sync-tmp.<pid>` next to the destination and renames it
# into place. A sync killed in between (a full disk did it here) leaves the temp
# behind, in a directory where SOURCE `.cargo/config.toml` files also live.
.*.nros-sync-tmp.[0-9]*

# THIRD writer, same class, and this one had four files ON MAIN since phase-402.
# `cargo-nano-ros`'s `atomic_file.rs` stages `.<name>.nros-tmp.<pid>` beside the
# destination and renames it into place; a build killed in between leaves it.
# The rules above did not catch these because the temps sit in `metadata/` and
# end in `.nros-tmp.<pid>` AFTER the `.unprobeable` suffix — so `*.unprobeable`
# missed them exactly the way `*.json` had missed `.json.unprobeable` one rule
# up. That is the same escape twice, on the same path, so this rule is written
# for the WRITER rather than for the directory: `atomic_file.rs` always uses the
# `.nros-tmp.` infix, so matching it anywhere closes the class instead of
# waiting for a fourth suffix to slip past a fourth pattern.
**/*.nros-tmp.[0-9]*

# RFC-0061 / phase-318 W1 — per-binary codegen-fingerprint cache.
.nros-cache/

# Local agent tooling (phase-321: swept in by a `git add -A`, never project files)
.agents/
skills-lock.json
third-party/ros/
.nros-box-tree

# The Zephyr lane's Python interpreter. `scripts/zephyr/setup.sh` deliberately
# does NOT install Python prerequisites — choosing between a distro package,
# `pip --user` and a venv is a decision about YOUR interpreter — and names this
# path as the one it picks up automatically (`nros_zephyr_activate`, resolved
# per-lane rather than on PATH). So the tree tells you to create it and then
# has to not track it.
scripts/zephyr/.venv/

# Generated by scripts/ci/runner-container.sh — regenerate, never edit or commit.
ci/docker/runner/

# Issue 0883/0884 follow-up — the generated open-issue list is an ARTIFACT.
#
# It was committed with `merge=union` so concurrent filings would not collide.
# That works locally and NOT where it mattered: GitHub computes mergeability and
# rebases queue entries SERVER-SIDE, where `.gitattributes` merge drivers do not
# run. Measured 2026-09-03 — a branch that rebased clean locally reported
# CONFLICTING on GitHub, on this file alone. Untracked removes the conflict
# class instead of mitigating it. Regenerate with
# `python3 scripts/gen-issue-index.py`; `just check issue-index` does it for you.
/docs/issues/open.md
# Generated by scripts/gen-pool-inventory.py and rebuilt by `just book`,
# `just check pool-inventory` and the docs workflow. Tracked until phase-418:
# every PR regenerated it, so it was the second file (after open.md) whose only
# role in a diff was to conflict and serialise the merge queue.
/book/src/reference/static-pool-inventory.md
# Generated by scripts/gen-config-surface.py — same class, same reason: it is
# regenerated by any PR that adds or removes a knob, so as a TRACKED file its
# only role in a diff is to conflict and serialise the merge queue.
/book/src/reference/configuration-surface.md
